--TEST--
Core\IO::writeStream accepts a stream exactly as long as `max` and refuses the one a byte past it, and the refused write leaves nothing on disk — the bound named on both sides, and the partial-file rule asserted by the `exists` after the throw rather than by the throw itself — `rule:core-classes/io-write-stream`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The same twelve bytes twice, under two ceilings one apart. A member that
// counted the wrong way round prints plausibly against either half alone: the
// accepted side and the refused side have to be named together for the bound to
// be pinned at all.
array<bytes> $chunks = ["aaaa" as bytes, "bbbb" as bytes, "cccc" as bytes];

Core\IO::writeStream("fits.txt", $chunks, {max: 12});
echo "fits: ", Core\IO::read("fits.txt"), "\n";

// `rule:core-classes/io-write-stream`'s second rule. Two of the three chunks had already reached the
// file when the third passed the ceiling, so a member that only threw would
// leave eight bytes behind that nothing ever wrote on purpose -- and the
// `exists` is the only thing that can tell that apart from this one.
try {
    Core\IO::writeStream("over.txt", $chunks, {max: 11});
    echo "a ceiling of eleven accepted twelve bytes\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    if (Core\IO::exists("over.txt")) {
        echo "refused, and the partial file was left behind\n";
    } else {
        echo "refused, and nothing was left behind\n";
    }
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
fits: aaaabbbbcccc
refused, and nothing was left behind
